from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "11. The End of the Sign-Program at Acts 28"),
("p", "Claim: the passages in 1 Corinthians 12 to 14 give tongues a purpose and a program, and the book of Acts gives the program its close. The volume sets the two side by side and states the ruling that follows. It states also what the ruling does not rest on, and where it is inference."),
("sub", "11.1 What the letter gives: a sign for a people"),
("p", "The letter supplies the purpose. Tongues are a sign to “them that believe not” (1 Corinthians 14:22), on the authority of a passage spoken to “this people” (1 Corinthians 14:21), whom Isaiah names as the people ruled from Jerusalem (Isaiah 28:14). A sign in Scripture is given to someone for something, as Volume 1 of this series read the passages. The sign of tongues is given to Israel, and its purpose is to mark the LORD’s speech to a people that would not hear."),
("p", "Paul states elsewhere that “the Jews require a sign” (1 Corinthians 1:22). The sentence is in the same letter and agrees with 14:22. Paul’s own preaching is “Christ crucified, unto the Jews a stumblingblock, and unto the Greeks foolishness” (1 Corinthians 1:23). The Jews ask for a sign, and the sign is for them. The Greeks seek wisdom."),
("sub", "11.2 What Acts gives: the sign-program to its last record"),
("p", "The book of Acts reports signs from Pentecost on. The last sign it reports is on Malta: Paul “prayed, and laid his hands on him, and healed him” (Acts 28:8), and “others also, which had diseases in the island, came, and were healed” (Acts 28:9). Volume 1 read the verses as the last sign the text records. The same chapter closes the book with a scene that sets Israel’s leaders under a verdict."),
("p", "At Rome Paul quotes the prophet. “Well spake the Holy Ghost by Esaias the prophet unto our fathers, Saying, Go unto this people, and say, Hearing ye shall hear, and shall not understand; and seeing ye shall see, and not perceive” (Acts 28:25, 26). He gives the reason from Isaiah: “For the heart of this people is waxed gross, and their ears are dull of hearing, and their eyes have they closed” (Acts 28:27). And he concludes: “Be it known therefore unto you, that the salvation of God is sent unto the Gentiles, and that they will hear it” (Acts 28:28)."),
("p", "The scene uses the same phrase as Isaiah 28 and 1 Corinthians 14:21: “this people.” The people who would not hear in Isaiah’s day are the people of whom Paul says at Rome that their ears are dull of hearing. The volume does not say that the two passages are identical. It says that the book of Acts closes with a verdict upon the same people to whom the sign of tongues was given, a verdict on their hearing, and that the salvation of God is then sent to the Gentiles. The national suspension of Israel’s Prophecy Program at Acts 28 is argued in Acts Commentary Volume 5. This volume takes the suspension as that volume sets it out and applies it to the question of the gifts."),
("sub", "11.3 The ruling"),
("p", "With the national suspension of Israel’s Prophecy Program at Acts 28, the sign-program associated with Israel ceases programmatically. Signs do not continue into the Mystery Program. The tongues of 1 Corinthians 14 were a sign to Israel and belonged to that program. They ceased with it, and no present practice of tongues is the gift the text describes. The sentence governs the programs. It makes no statement about God’s power."),
("p", "The ruling rests on three passages: 1 Corinthians 14:21, 22, which gives the purpose and the people; Isaiah 28:11, 12, which fixes the people; and Acts 28:25–28, which reports the verdict on the people and the sending of the salvation of God to the Gentiles. It does not rest on a decision about “that which is perfect.” If the perfect is the return of Christ, the ruling stands, because the sign-program has already ended at the suspension; and if it is something else, the ruling stands for the same reason."),
("sub", "11.4 Prophecy"),
("p", "The ruling on prophecy is an inference, and the volume says so. No verse says that prophecy ended at Acts 28. Three things support the inference. First, 13:8 states that prophecies shall fail, and prophecy stands in 13:9 with knowledge as “in part.” Second, 14:30 describes prophecy as a word “revealed,” given in the moment to a member of the assembly. Third, prophecy in the New Testament is joined to the signs and gifts of the years Acts records. It appears with tongues at Ephesus, it appears in the company of Paul at Caesarea, and Paul sets it beside tongues in the assembly. The volume holds that a revealed word of the kind described belongs to the same period and the same program as the other gifts. If a reader holds that prophecy continues, the objection is answered in chapter 13."),
("sub", "11.5 Paul’s signs and the overlap"),
("p", "The Corinthian gifts and Paul’s own signs in Acts 9 to 28 are overlap phenomena. The volume reports them as the text reports them. It does not assign them to Israel’s program, and it does not convert them into the Mystery. What it argues is the purpose of tongues from 14:21, 22 and the end of the sign-program at Acts 28. Paul’s apostleship is not changed by the argument: he is the apostle of the Gentiles, his gospel is the gospel of the grace of God, and where he reasons with Israel from Israel’s scriptures he proclaims Israel’s Messiah and kingdom hope to Israel. That is audience-specific preaching, and not a change of commission."),
("sub", "11.6 Where tongues and prophecy are named in Paul’s letters"),
("p", "Tongues are named in Paul’s letters only in 1 Corinthians 12 to 14. The other lists of gifts do not name them. Romans 12 lists prophecy, ministry, teaching, exhortation, giving, ruling and mercy: “Having then gifts differing according to the grace that is given to us, whether prophecy, let us prophesy according to the proportion of faith” (Romans 12:6). Ephesians 4:11 lists apostles, prophets, evangelists, pastors and teachers. Prophecy appears in 1 Thessalonians 5:20 and in the two letters to Timothy. The volume reports the pattern and draws no conclusion from silence. It notes that the passage in which tongues are named is also the passage in which Paul gives their purpose from the law, and that the purpose points to a people who are not the subject of Romans 12 or of Ephesians 4."),
("tbl", "Where the two gifts are named in Paul’s letters.", [
    ("Romans 12:6", T("Rom 12:6"), "Prophecy is listed; tongues are not."),
    ("1 Corinthians 12:10", T("1 Cor 12:10"), "Prophecy, tongues and interpretation are listed."),
    ("Ephesians 4:11", T("Eph 4:11"), "Prophets are listed among the gifts of the ascended Christ; tongues are not."),
    ("1 Thessalonians 5:20", T("1 Thess 5:20"), "Prophesyings are not to be despised."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("ctbl", "The ruling and its grounds.", [
    ("Point", "The text", "What it gives"),
    ("The purpose of tongues", "1 Corinthians 14:22", "A sign to them that believe not"),
    ("The people of the sign", "Isaiah 28:11, 12, 14; 1 Corinthians 14:21", "This people, ruled from Jerusalem"),
    ("The verdict on the people", "Acts 28:25–27", "Their ears dull of hearing, quoted from Isaiah"),
    ("The sending to the Gentiles", "Acts 28:28", "The salvation of God is sent unto the Gentiles"),
    ("The end of the sign-program", "Acts 28 read with Acts Volume 5", "The sign-program associated with Israel ceases programmatically"),
    ("The end of prophecy", "1 Corinthians 13:8; 14:30", "Inferred; no verse says prophecy ended at Acts 28"),
  ], [0.28, 0.34, 0.38]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Tongues are a sign to Israel by Paul’s own quotation of the law, and the sign belongs to the sign-program.",
    "The last sign recorded in Acts is on Malta, and the book then closes on the verdict of Isaiah 6 and the sending of the salvation of God to the Gentiles.",
    "With the national suspension of Israel’s Prophecy Program at Acts 28 the sign-program associated with Israel ceases programmatically, and signs do not continue into the Mystery Program.",
    "No present practice of tongues is the gift the text describes.",
    "The ruling on prophecy is an inference from 13:8, 14:30 and the sign-program, and the volume says that no verse states it.",
  ]),
]
